['Here is an example of how you might create such an API using ASP.NET Core WebAPI and Entity Framework Core to manage your roles. 

First, let's define our Role model:

```csharp
public class Role
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public bool IsActive { get; set; } = true;
}
```

Then, let's implement the API methods:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

[ApiController]
public class RolesController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly ApplicationDbContext _context;
    private readonly ILogger<RolesController> _logger;

    public RolesController(ApplicationDbContext context, ILogger<RolesController> logger)
    {
        _context = context;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPut("{id}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> UpdateRole(int id, Role role)
    {
        if (id != role.Id)
            return BadRequest();

        try
        {
            _context.Entry(role).State = EntityState.Modified;
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

            _logger.LogInformation("Updated Role: {0}", role);
        }
        catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex)
        {
            if (!RoleExists(id))
                return NotFound();

            throw;
        }

        return NoContent();
    }

    [HttpPatch("{id}/archive")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> ArchiveRole(int id)
    {
        var role = await _context.Roles.FindAsync(id);

        if (role == null)
            return NotFound();

        role.IsActive = false;
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

        _logger.LogInformation("Archived Role: {0}", role);

        return NoContent();
    }

    private bool RoleExists(int id) =>
        _context.Roles.Any(e => e.Id == id);
}
```
This code assumes that you have a `ApplicationDbContext` class with an appropriate DbSet for roles, and a logging mechanism in place (for instance, using Serilog or NLog). It also uses the built-in dependency injection provided by ASP.NET Core. 

Please make sure to replace `RoleExists(id)` method call with your own check for existence of role as this is just an example. Also don't forget to add exception handling, authentication/authorization and other necessary code based on your requirements in production ready solution. 

Make sure you handle concurrency issues properly. In this case, a `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` is thrown if the client tries to update a role that has been updated by another client since it was last fetched from the database. It's important to catch and handle these exceptions in your API methods. 

This code also logs each operation using an `ILogger` instance, which is configured separately depending on your application setup (for example, you might use a logging library like Serilog or NLog).
']

['Here is a code snippet implementing your request:

```C#
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using System.Linq;

public class XmlParser
{
    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> NamespaceMapping = new Dictionary<string, string>();

    public List<XPathNavigator> ParseDocument(string xmlContent, string elementName)
    {
        try
        {
            var settings = new XmlReaderSettings()
            {
                DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Parse,
                IgnoreWhitespace = true,
            };
            using (var reader = XmlReader.Create(new System.IO.StringReader(xmlContent), settings))
            {
                var document = new XPathDocument(reader);
                var navigator = document.CreateNavigator();
                return GetElementsByName(navigator, elementName).ToList();
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("An error occurred while parsing the XML: " + ex.Message);
            throw;
        }
    }

    private IEnumerable<XPathNavigator> GetElementsByName(XPathNavigator navigator, string name)
    {
        var iterator = navigator.SelectDescendants(name, NamespaceMapping).GetEnumerator();
        while (iterator.MoveNext())
            yield return iterator.Current;
    }
}
```
This code uses an XPathDocument to parse the XML document with DTD processing enabled and ignoring whitespace for efficient memory usage. It also provides a method that yields each matching element as it is found, avoiding loading all matching elements into memory at once. The function handles exceptions by rethrowing them so they can be caught in the calling code.

To handle different namespace mappings gracefully you need to define your own `NamespaceMapping` dictionary where keys are prefixes and values are URIs (e.g., {"ns", "http://example.com/namespace"}). This ensures that elements from specified namespaces are also considered if they match the name. 

The XmlReader with DtdProcessing set to Parse will attempt to parse as much of the document as possible without throwing an exception when it encounters malformed sections, and will continue processing the rest of the document if a section cannot be parsed correctly. This can be changed according to your requirements. 

The function efficiently manages memory usage by streaming the XML document through the XPathDocument parser rather than loading the entire document into memory at once, which can save significant amounts of memory for large XML files. However, it's important to note that this only aids in handling larger XML files and doesn't solve any fundamental limitations inherent to parsing XML documents.
']

