['Here is the Java code implementing your request:
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Objects;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final Map<String, MoSTState> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private static int maxCacheSize = 100; // default maximum size
    private static LinkedList<String> lruQueue = new LinkedList<>(); 

    public static void configure(int maxCacheSize) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(maxCacheSize, "Maximum cache size cannot be null");
        StateLoader.maxCacheSize = maxCacheSize;
    }

    public static MoSTState loadStateFromFile(String filePath) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        Objects.requireNonNull(filePath, "File path cannot be null");

        // check cache first
        synchronized (cache) {
            if (cache.containsKey(filePath)) {
                lruQueue.remove(filePath);
                lruQueue.addLast(filePath);
                return cache.get(filePath);
            }
        }

        // load from file
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            MoSTState state = null;
            boolean objectReadSuccessfully = false;

            while (!objectReadSuccessfully && ois.available() > 0) {
                Object obj = ois.readObject();

                if (obj instanceof MoSTState) {
                    state = (MoSTState) obj;
                    objectReadSuccessfully = true;
                }
            }

            // add to cache and evict old entries if necessary
            synchronized (cache) {
                if (objectReadSuccessfully) {
                    cache.put(filePath, state);
                    lruQueue.addLast(filePath);

                    while (lruQueue.size() > maxCacheSize) {
                        String evictedFilePath = lruQueue.removeFirst();
                        cache.remove(evictedFilePath);
                    }
                }
            }

            return objectReadSuccessfully ? state : null;
        }
    }
}
```
In this code:
- A `ConcurrentHashMap` is used to store loaded states in the cache, ensuring thread safety.
- The `LinkedList` `lruQueue` is used as a queue to track the order of recently accessed file paths. When a state is accessed, its path is moved to the end of this list.
- If the size of the `cache` exceeds `maxCacheSize`, the least recently used item (front of `lruQueue`) is removed from both the cache and `lruQueue`.
- The method `loadStateFromFile()` first checks the cache to see if a state for the given file path already exists. If not, it attempts to load the state from the file, adding it to the cache if successful.
- The methods accept a `filePath` parameter and ensure this is not null.
- The method throws an `IOException` in case of issues with reading/writing files and a `ClassNotFoundException` if the serialized class of a serialized object cannot be found. You may want to handle these exceptions differently in your application as per requirement.

Please note, you need to replace the `MoSTState` with your actual state class name. And this will work for single threaded environment. For multithreaded environments it's better to use a concurrent map like `ConcurrentHashMap`.
']

