['Here's a code implementation for your requirements using C language, but be aware that this is quite complex and has some edge cases which are not fully covered by this basic example (like handling child processes crashes). Also, it uses POSIX system calls to illustrate the concept, so you will need a POSIX compatible operating system:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// Global variables for thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock;
FILE* logFile = NULL;
int childPIDs[1024] = { 0 }; // Assuming max number of processes is 1024
int childrenCount = 0;

void handleChildTermination(int signalNumber) {
    int status;
    wait(&status);
}

void runCommandWithTimeout(char* cmd[], char* logPath, char* envVarsSource[]) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock to ensure thread safety

    pid_t childPid = fork();
    if (childPid == 0) { // Child process
        for (int i = 0; envVarsSource[i] != NULL; ++i) {
            putenv(envVarsSource[i]);
        }

        time_t startTime = time(NULL);
        execvp(cmd[0], cmd); // Run command

        // If control reaches here, an error occurred during the execution
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock mutex before exiting
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    } else if (childPid < 0) { // Fork failed
        fprintf(logFile, "Failed to fork: %s\n", strerror(errno));

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock mutex before exiting
        return;
    } else { // Parent process
        childPIDs[childrenCount++] = childPid;
        signal(SIGCHLD, handleChildTermination); // Setup signal handler to clean up child processes

        alarm(5); // Set an alarm for 5 seconds
        int status = 0;
        waitpid(childPid, &status, WNOHANG | WUNTRACED | WCONTINUED); // Wait for the child process to terminate or exceed its time limit

        time_t endTime = time(NULL);
        if (WIFEXITED(status)) { // Child exited normally
            fprintf(logFile, "'%s' ran successfully from %ld to %ld\n", cmd[0], startTime, endTime);
        } else if (WIFSIGNALED(status)) { // Child terminated by a signal
            fprintf(logFile, "'%s' was killed by signal: %d\n", cmd[0], WTERMSIG(status));
        }

        alarm(0); // Clear the alarm
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock mutex before exiting
}

void killAllChildren() {
    for (int i = 0; i < childrenCount; ++i) {
        if (childPIDs[i] > 0) {
            kill(childPIDs[i], SIGKILL); // Kill all child processes
        }
    }
}
```
In the `runCommandWithTimeout` function, it forks a new process and executes the command. The parent process sets an alarm to terminate after 5 seconds if the child process doesn't complete within that time. The child process cleans up its own resources before exiting or being terminated by a signal. If a fork fails, an error message is written to the log file.

In the `killAllChildren` function, it sends a SIGKILL to all child processes, ensuring they are cleaned up properly if needed.

Note: This code doesn't handle race conditions or data corruption in the log file when multiple threads call the `runCommandWithTimeout` function simultaneously. It also assumes that there will be no more than 1024 concurrent processes and all strings passed to functions are null-terminated. For a production-grade solution, you should use appropriate error handling and better synchronization mechanisms (like semaphores or file locks) to handle these situations properly.
']

