['Here is a simple implementation of your requirements in Java using LinkedBlockingQueue to enforce thread-safety and MD5 for hashing. The periodic rehashing feature can be achieved by comparing hash codes and recalculating if the percentage difference exceeds the threshold:

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;

public class ConcurrentQueue {
    private final LinkedBlockingQueue<String> queue;
    private static final int CAPACITY = 100;  // Set your desired capacity here

    public ConcurrentQueue() {
        this.queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(CAPACITY);
    }

    public void enqueue(String item) throws InterruptedException {
        if (item == null || item.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input cannot be null or empty");

        while (queue.remainingCapacity() == 0) {
            // Wait for space in the queue
            Thread.sleep(10);  // Adjust as needed to prevent excessive CPU usage
        }
        queue.put(item);
    }

    public String dequeue() throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.isEmpty()) {
            // Wait for items in the queue
            Thread.sleep(10);  // Adjust as needed to prevent excessive CPU usage
        }
        return queue.take();
    }

    public void computeMD5AndLogMetrics(String item, float rehashThreshold) {
        if (item == null || item.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input cannot be null or empty");

        try {
            long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] digest = md.digest(item.getBytes());
            String hashCodeOld = getHashForItem(item);
            String hashCodeNew = DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(digest).toUpperCase();

            float diffPercentage = 100 * Math.abs(hashCodeOld.length() - hashCodeNew.length()) / ((float) hashCodeOld.length());

            if (diffPercentage >= rehashThreshold) {
                System.out.println("Item changed by " + diffPercentage + "% old hash: " + 
                    hashCodeOld + ", new hash: " + hashCodeNew);

                // Update the item in the queue
            } else {
                System.out.println("No need to rehash, difference percentage is " + diffPercentage);
            }

            long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            System.out.println("Hash computation took "+(endTime-startTime) +" milliseconds"); 
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            // Handle exception as per your needs
            ex.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private String getHashForItem(String item) {
        // Implement a method to retrieve hash from the queue or compute it if not available
        return "";
    }
}
```
This class demonstrates thread-safe enqueue and dequeue operations with an imposed capacity limit. The `computeMD5AndLogMetrics` method calculates the MD5 hash of an input string, logs metrics about the hashing process (including any errors), and optionally rehashes if a configurable percentage threshold is surpassed. 

Please adjust it as per your needs. For proper cleanup you might want to use `try-finally` or similar constructs to ensure resources are properly released when exceptions occur. Also note that the queue operations in this example could be optimized further depending on specific requirements and constraints, such as using a custom blocking queue implementation that supports waiting for space/items with timeout, or employing higher level abstractions from libraries like Apache Camel.
