import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;

public class MD5HashCalculator {
    private static final int BUFFER_SIZE = 8192; // Optimal buffer size for disk I/O and CPU efficiency.
    private static ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool(); // Thread pool for concurrency.

    public Future<String> calculateMD5Hash(String filePath) {
        return executor.submit(() -> {
            try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(filePath)) {
                MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");

                byte[] buffer = new byte[BUFFER_SIZE];
                int bytesRead;
                while ((bytesRead = fis.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                    md.update(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
                }

                byte[] digestBytes = md.digest();
                return DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(digestBytes).toLowerCase(); // Converting to hexadecimal string.
            } catch (IOException | NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                System.err.println("Error while reading/hashing file: " + filePath);
                e.printStackTrace();
                return null; // Returning null on error.
            }
        });
    }
}
```
This code implements an efficient MD5 hash calculation method using a fixed-size buffer for both I/O and CPU efficiency, handling exceptions by logging the error and returning null to indicate failure. The hashing is done concurrently with `ExecutorService` for maximum throughput. This design allows multiple files to be processed simultaneously without conflicts or data corruption, providing a significant performance boost in high-load situations.
