void* reallocate_with_check(size_t new_size) {
    void* ptr = realloc(conn_records, new_size * sizeof(TCP_CONNECTION_RECORD));
    if (!ptr) {
        return handle_allocation_failure();
    }
    return ptr;
}

// Thread-safe function to add a connection.
int conn_add(const TCP_CONNECTION_RECORD* record) {
    // Lock for writing.
    if (pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&rwlock)) {
        log_error("Could not obtain write lock");
        return -1;
    }

    pthread_spin_lock(&alloc_spinlock);

    // Allocate more memory for the array.
    if (conn_record_count == M) {
        M *= 2; // Grow the size by 2x for now.
        conn_records = reallocate_with_check(M);
    }

    pthread_spin_unlock(&alloc_spinlock);

    // Add the new record.
    conn_records[conn_record_count++] = *record;

    if (pthread_rwlock_unlock(&rwlock)) {
        log_error("Could not release write lock");
        return -1;
    }

    return 0; // Success.
}
```

The above code uses a reader-writer lock for concurrency control and a spinlock for dynamic memory allocation to prevent multiple threads from allocating memory simultaneously, which could lead to contention and memory shortages. The function `conn_add` is thread safe in this regard. Please note that the full implementation would be more complex including integration with external systems (API calls etc.) and error handling in different scenarios. It would also require extensive unit testing and profiling to ensure performance optimization strategies are effective.
']

