["Here's an efficient Java solution using a PriorityQueue to merge multiple sorted lists in a single sorted list. The custom comparator can be defined by implementing the Comparator interface. This function efficiently handles edge cases such as empty lists or duplicate elements, while also avoiding unnecessary object allocations for efficiency and optimal garbage collection.

```java
import java.util.*;

class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode(int x) {
        val = x;
        next = null;
    }
}

public class Solution {
    public static ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
        PriorityQueue<ListNode> queue = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a.val - b.val);

        for (ListNode node : lists) {
            if (node != null) {
                queue.add(node);
            }
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode current = dummy;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            ListNode node = queue.poll();

            if (node.next != null) {
                queue.add(node.next);
            }

            current.next = node;
            current = current.next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

This function merges multiple sorted lists into a single linked list. The PriorityQueue ensures that the smallest element is always at the head of the queue, which simplifies the process of finding the next node to add to the merged list. By removing and adding nodes from/to the queue as needed, we can efficiently merge all nodes while avoiding unnecessary object allocations or deallocations.
"]

