["Here's a high-level pseudo code for this problem using Java:

```java
import java.util.*;

class UserProfile {
  String userId;
  Map<String, Double> interests; // where key is interest and value is relevance score
}

class Cluster {
  Set<UserProfile> members = new HashSet<>();

  public void add(UserProfile profile) {
    this.members.add(profile);
  }

  public int size() {
    return this.members.size();
  }
}

class ClusteringService {
  private Map<String, Cluster> clusters = new HashMap<>(); // key is interest

  public void addUserProfile(UserProfile profile) {
    boolean addedToCluster = false;

    for (Map.Entry<String, Double> entry : profile.interests.entrySet()) {
      if (clusters.containsKey(entry.getKey())) {
        clusters.get(entry.getKey()).add(profile);
        addedToCluster = true;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!addedToCluster) {
      Cluster newCluster = new Cluster();
      newCluster.add(profile);

      for (Map.Entry<String, Double> entry : profile.interests.entrySet()) {
        clusters.put(entry.getKey(), newCluster);
      }
    }
  }

  public int getClusterSizeByInterest(String interest) {
    if (clusters.containsKey(interest)) {
      return clusters.get(interest).size();
    } else {
      return 0;
    }
  }
}
```

Here's what each part of the code does:
1. `UserProfile` represents a user profile with a unique ID and a map of interests to relevance scores.
2. `Cluster` is a simple class that holds a set of UserProfiles and counts them as a cluster. 
