['Here is an example of how you might implement this in Java using RabbitMQ for the messaging service and Guava's EventBus to handle listeners. This solution does not include all the code necessary to set up a complete, working system but it shows some of the key components. 

```java
import com.google.common.eventbus.*;
import com.rabbitmq.client.*;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class MessageQueueSystem {
    private final ConnectionFactory factory = new ConnectionFactory();
    private Connection connection;
    private Channel channel;
    private EventBus eventBus;

    public void initialize(String host, String username, String password) throws IOException, TimeoutException {
        factory.setHost(host);
        factory.setUsername(username);
        factory.setPassword(password);

        connection = factory.newConnection();
        channel = connection.createChannel();
        eventBus = new EventBus("event-bus");
    }

    public void registerListener(Object listener) {
        eventBus.register(listener);
    }

    public void unregisterListener(Object listener) {
        eventBus.unregister(listener);
    }

    public void publishEvent(MessageEvent event) throws IOException {
        channel.basicPublish("", "events", null, event.getBody().getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    }

    @Subscribe
    public void handleEvent(MessageEvent event) {
        System.out.println("Received event: " + event);
    }
}
```
In this code, `initialize` method sets up the RabbitMQ connection and channel which are used to publish events. The `registerListener` and `unregisterListener` methods manage subscription states for different listeners.

Note that a production-ready system would require more robust handling of failures, optimizations for varying load conditions, and more complex event handling (e.g., with more than one queue and multiple consumers). It is also recommended to use connection pooling, as opening a new RabbitMQ connection for each message can be quite expensive in terms of resources.
']

