['Here is a sample code implementing all the requirements you mentioned above in C language. Please note that handling thread safety and file pointer validity are platform specific and this code assumes a POSIX-compliant environment.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    FILE* log_file;
} LogConfig;

static pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

char *processString(const char *input, size_t limit, FILE *fp, const LogConfig *logCfg) {
    if (!input || !logCfg || !logCfg->log_file || !fp) return NULL; 

    size_t len = strlen(input);
    if (len >= limit) {
        fprintf(logCfg->log_file, "Error: Input string exceeds the size limit.\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    char *newStr = malloc(limit + 1); 

    if (!newStr) {
        fprintf(logCfg->log_file, "Memory allocation failure\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    strncpy(newStr, input, limit);
    newStr[limit] = '\0';

    if (len < limit - 1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

        fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);
        if (ftell(fp) == -1L) {
            fprintf(logCfg->log_file, "Invalid file pointer\n");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            free(newStr);
            return NULL;
        } 

        newStr[len] = '/';
        newStr[limit] = '\0';

        fprintf(fp, "%s", newStr);

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    } else {
        fprintf(logCfg->log_file, "Info: Slash not appended. String length equals or exceeds size limit.\n"); 
    }

    fprintf(logCfg->log_file, "Initial string length: %lu\nFinal string length: %lu\nSlash appended: %s\n", 
            strlen(input), len < limit - 1 ? len + 1 : len, len < limit - 1 ? "Yes" : "No");

    return newStr;
}
```
In this code, the `LogConfig` structure contains a file pointer to the log file. The function `processString` takes a string, a size limit, a file pointer and a configuration structure as parameters. It validates input parameters before proceeding with processing. If the input string exceeds the size limit, an error message is logged to the provided log file and the function returns NULL. 

If the string length is less than the limit minus one, a slash is appended to it using a platform-specific `fseek` and `ftell` functions to validate the underlying resource of the file pointer. The dynamically allocated memory for the resulting string is locked with a mutex before writing to the file, ensuring thread safety in multi-threaded environments.

Finally, an informative message is logged detailing initial string length, final string length and whether slash was appended or not using `fprintf` function. The function concludes by returning a pointer to the new string. 

Please remember that this code requires you to link against pthread library due to use of mutex for thread safety. If your platform does not support mutexes, you would have to replace it with an appropriate synchronization mechanism or remove if the function is going to be used in a single-threaded environment only.
