['Here is a high-level Java implementation of your task using ExecutorService for managing threads and ConcurrentLinkedQueue for maintaining file processing order in case of interruption. Batch size and thread pool size are dynamically adjusted based on available memory. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;

public class FileProcessor {
    private static final int MAX_RETRY = 5;

    public void processFiles(File[] files, Function<String, String> transformation) throws InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService executor = createExecutor();

        ConcurrentLinkedQueue<Future<?>> futures = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();
        for (File file : files) {
            futures.add(executor.submit(() -> processFile(file, transformation)));
        }

        while (!futures.isEmpty()) {
            Iterator<Future<?>> it = futures.iterator();
            while (it.hasNext()) {
                Future<?> future = it.next();
                if (future.isDone() || future.isCancelled()) {
                    try {
                        future.get(); // Handle exceptions
                        it.remove();
                    } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                        System.out.println("Error processing file: " + e.getMessage());
                    } finally {
                        executor.execute(() -> processFile(file, transformation)); // Retry on failure
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }

    private void processFile(File file, Function<String, String> transformation) {
        Path path = Paths.get(file.toURI());
        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path);
             BufferedWriter writer = Files.newBufferedWriter(Paths.get(path.toString() + ".out"))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                String transformed = transformation.apply(line); // apply transformations
                if (StringUtils.isNotBlank(transformed)) {
                    writer.write(transformed + "\n");
                }
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error reading/writing file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    private ExecutorService createExecutor() {
        int availableProcessors = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        long maxMemory = Runtime.getRuntime().maxMemory();

        // Dynamically adjust thread pool size based on memory and CPU resources
        int threadPoolSize = (int) Math.min(availableProcessors, maxMemory / 1024 / 1024 * 5);
        return Executors.newFixedThreadPool(threadPoolSize);
    }
}
```
In order to support custom transformations and batch processing, you will need a mechanism for reading this configuration from a file or other source, parsing it into Function objects that can be applied in processFile method, as well as adjusting the buffer size of BufferedReader based on the configured batch size. 

This code doesn't cover all the detailed requirements such as edge case handling, logging and graceful interruption handling. You would need to enhance this code according to your needs. Please note that for real-world scenarios consider using proven frameworks or libraries for these purposes, like Spring Batch, Apache Nifi etc.
