['Here's the implementation of your task using Java. I use `MessageDigest` to calculate MD5 hash and `AtomicInteger` to track the number of computations done so far:

```java
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class MD5HashGenerator {
    private static final AtomicInteger computationCounter = new AtomicInteger(0);
    private static final int MAX_LENGTH = 1024;

    // Assuming you have a logger configured in your application for debug level logging.
    // If not, you need to configure and use one.
    Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MD5HashGenerator.class);

    public String computeMD5Hash(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.length() > MAX_LENGTH) {
            return "";
        }

        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));

            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (byte b : hashBytes) {
                sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
            }

            int computationCount = computationCounter.incrementAndGet();
            logger.debug("Computation #{}: input '{}', output '{}'", computationCount, input, sb);

            return sb.toString();
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            // You can log and handle this exception as needed
            e.printStackTrace();
            logger.error("MD5 algorithm not found.", e);
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```
This class should integrate with your existing logging framework, where each hash computation logs the input and the resulting hash at a debug level. If an exception occurs during MD5 hashing (like no MD5 algorithm found), it will log that as well. The method also validates that the input string does not exceed 1024 characters.

The `AtomicInteger` is used for thread-safe computation counting since incrementing a counter is not an atomic operation in Java by default, and other threads may interfere with each other if they try to read/write at the same time.
']

