["This is a complex task that involves managing a linked list with thread safety, random content generation, filtering and dynamic memory allocation. For brevity, I'll only provide a simplified example of how you might implement these features in C. Here are the key parts of such functions/structures involved:

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct LogEventRecord {
    time_t timestamp;
    int eventType; // Change this to an enum if required
    char* message;
    struct LogEventRecord *next;
} LOG_EVENT_RECORD;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static LOG_EVENT_RECORD *head = NULL; // This will be our linked list

// Function to add a record, thread safe with locks
void addRecord(LOG_EVENT_RECORD* newRecord) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL) {
        head = newRecord;
    } else {
        LOG_EVENT_RECORD *current = head;
        while(current->next != NULL) {
            current = current->next;
        }
        current->next = newRecord;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Function to free all memory, thread safe with locks
void freeList() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    LOG_EVENT_RECORD *current = head;
    while(current != NULL) {
        LOG_EVENT_RECORD *next = current->next;
        free(current->message);
        free(current);
        current = next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
The `addRecord` function adds a record to the end of our linked list. The `freeList` function frees all memory associated with each node and the entire list itself. Both these functions are thread-safe due to the use of mutual exclusion locks (mutexes). 

For your second requirement, you can implement the function as follows:
```C
#include <stdio.h>
