['Here's a simplified version of a C program for your requirements using `getopt` for command line argument parsing and `fopen`, `fgets`, and `fclose` for file handling. The logging functionality is not included since it can be quite complex and may vary depending on the specifics of your project.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <getopt.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

void printUsage() {
    printf("Usage: program [options] file1 [file2...]\n");
    printf("Options:\n");
    printf(" -c to count the number of non-empty lines in the input file.\n");
    printf(" -s to calculate the total number of characters in the file.\n");
    printf(" -o output_file to redirect the output to the specified file instead of printing to the standard output.\n");
    printf(" --manpage to display comprehensive documentation about all supported options and their functionalities.\n");
}

void processFile(char *filename, int countNonEmptyLines, int countCharacters) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file %s\n", filename);
        return;
    }

    int lineCount = 0;
    int nonEmptyLineCount = 0;
    int characterCount = 0;

    const size_t LINE_SIZE = 512;
    char *line = malloc(LINE_SIZE);
    while (fgets(line, LINE_SIZE, file) != NULL) {
        lineCount++;
        if (!isspace(*line)) {
            nonEmptyLineCount++;
        }
        if (countCharacters) {
            characterCount += strlen(line);
        }
    }
    free(line);

    printf("File: %s\n", filename);
    printf("Lines: %d\n", lineCount);
    if (countNonEmptyLines) {
        printf("Non-empty lines: %d\n", nonEmptyLineCount);
    }
    if (countCharacters) {
        printf("Characters: %d\n", characterCount);
    }
    fclose(file);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    int countNonEmptyLines = 0;
    int countCharacters = 0;
    char *outputFile = NULL;
    FILE *outFile = stdout;

    static struct option long_options[] = {
        {"manpage", no_argument, 0, 'm'},
        {0, 0, 0, 0}
    };

    int c;
    while ((c = getopt_long(argc, argv, "cos:o:", long_options, NULL)) != -1) {
        switch (c) {
            case 'm':
                printUsage();
                return 0;
            case 'c':
                countNonEmptyLines = 1;
                break;
            case 's':
                countCharacters = 1;
                break;
            case 'o':
                outputFile = optarg;
                outFile = fopen(outputFile, "w");
                if (outFile == NULL) {
                    printf("Failed to open output file %s\n", outputFile);
                    return 1;
                }
                break;
            default:
                printUsage();
                return 1;
        }
    }

    // Handle non-option arguments (filenames)
    if (optind >= argc) {
        printUsage();
        return 1;
    }

    for (int index = optind; index < argc; index++) {
        processFile(argv[index], countNonEmptyLines, countCharacters);
    }

    if (outputFile != NULL) {
        fclose(outFile);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This program can be improved in many ways, such as adding error checking for `malloc` and `fopen` calls, handling command line options properly when they are not provided, adding a logging mechanism, etc. For a complete solution you would need to consider all of these factors and more depending on your specific needs.
']

['Here is a Java method named `calculateSHA256` which does exactly what you described.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class HashCalculator {

    public static String calculateSHA256(String filePath) {
        MessageDigest digest = null;
        try {
            digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            // This should never happen
            throw new RuntimeException("Unsupported hashing algorithm", e);
        }

        Path path = Paths.get(filePath);
        try (InputStream inputStream = Files.newInputStream(path)) {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[8192]; // Arbitrary chosen size
            int bytesRead;

            while ((bytesRead = inputStream.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                digest.update(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to read file " + path, e);
        }

        byte[] hash = digest.digest();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hash) {
            // Convert the signed byte to an unsigned int before formatting as hexadecimal string
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
This method reads a file in chunks of up to 8192 bytes and updates the hash digest with each chunk. It then converts each byte of the resulting hash to a hexadecimal string before returning it. If anything goes wrong (like an I/O error or unsupported hashing algorithm), it throws a `RuntimeException`, which you should handle in your application.
']

